      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Conformance",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-019-2-conformance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As well as sections marked as non-normative, all authoring guidelines, diagrams, examples, and notes in this specification are non-normative.\nEverything else in this specification is normative.\nThe key words MAY, MUST, MUST NOT, RECOMMENDED, SHOULD, and SHOULD NOT in this document are to be interpreted as described in\nBCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.\nA JSON-LD document complies with this specification if it follows the normative statements in appendix § 9. JSON-LD Grammar. JSON documents\ncan be interpreted as JSON-LD by following the n",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-020",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Basic Concepts",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-020-3-basic-concepts.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Example 2: Sample JSON document\n{\n  \"name\": \"Manu Sporny\",\n  \"homepage\": \"http://manu.sporny.org/\",\n  \"image\": \"http://manu.sporny.org/images/manu.png\"\n}\nInput\nExample 3: Sample JSON-LD document using full IRIs instead of terms\nExpanded (Input) \nStatements \nTurtle (Result) Open in playground\n{\n  \"http://schema.org/name\": \"Manu Sporny\",\n  \"http://schema.org/url\": {\n    \"@id\": \"http://manu.sporny.org/\"\n    ↑ The '@id' keyword means 'This value is an identifier that is an IRI'\n  },\nIn the example above, every property is unambiguously identified by an IRI and all values representing IRIs are expl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-021",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "IRIs",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-021-32-iris.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Contexts can either be directly embedded into the document (an embedded context) or be referenced using a URL. Assuming the context document in\nthe previous example can be retrieved at https://json-ld.org/contexts/person.jsonld, it can be referenced by adding a single line and allows a\nJSON-LD document to be expressed much more concisely as shown in the example below:\n  \"http://schema.org/image\": {\n    \"@id\": \"http://manu.sporny.org/images/manu.png\"\n  }\n}",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-022",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The Context",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-022-31-the-context.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Context\nExample 4: Context for the sample document in the previous section\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"name\": \"http://schema.org/name\",\n    ↑ This means that 'name' is shorthand for 'http://schema.org/name'\n    \"image\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://schema.org/image\",\n      ↑ This means that 'image' is shorthand for 'http://schema.org/image'\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n      ↑ This means that a string value associated with 'image'\n        should be interpreted as an identifier that is an IRI\n    },\n    \"homepage\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://schema.org/url\",\n      ↑ This means that 'homepage' is shorthand for 'http:/",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
