        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Imported Contexts",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 30,
      "target": "../sections/sec-044-4110-imported-contexts.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Example 50: A remote context to be imported in a type-scoped context\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"Type1\": \"http://example.com/vocab/Type1\",\n    \"Type2\": \"http://example.com/vocab/Type2\",\n    \"term1\": \"http://example.com/vocab#term1\",\n    \"term2\": \"http://example.com/vocab#term2\",\n    ...\n  }\n}\nExample 51: Sourcing a context in a type-scoped context and setting it to propagate\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"@version\": 1.1,\n    \"MyType\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://example.com/vocab#MyType\",\nThe effect would be the same as if the entire imported context had been copied into the type-scoped context:\nSimilarly, the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-045",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Protected Term Definitions",
      "pageStart": 30,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-045-4111-protected-term-definitions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "JSON-LD is used in many specifications as the specified data format. However, there is also a desire to allow some JSON-LD contents to be\nprocessed as plain JSON, without using any of the JSON-LD algorithms. Because JSON-LD is very flexible, some terms from the original format\nmay be locally overridden through the use of embedded contexts, and take a different meaning for JSON-LD based implementations. On the other\nhand, \"plain JSON\" implementations may not be able to interpret these embedded contexts, and hence will still interpret those terms with their\noriginal meaning. To prevent this dive",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-046",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-046-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "\"Person\": \"http://schema.org/Person\",\n      \"name\": \"http://schema.org/name\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"id\": \"https://digitalbazaar.com/\",\n  \"type\": \"Organization\",\n  \"member\" : {\n    \"id\": \"http://manu.sporny.org/about#manu\",\n    \"type\": \"Person\",\n    \"name\": \"Manu Sporny\"\n  }\n}\nInput\nExample 58: overriding permitted in property scoped context\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": [\n    {\n      – This context reflects the specification used by \"plain JSON\" implementations\n      \"@version\": 1.1,\n      \"@protected\": true,\n      \"Organization\": \"http://sc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-047",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-047-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "By preventing terms from being overridden, protection also prevents any adaptation of a term (e.g., defining a more precise datatype, restricting the\nterm's use to lists, etc.). This kind of adaptation is frequent with some general purpose contexts, for which protection would therefore hinder their\nusability. As a consequence, context publishers should use this feature with care.\nNote\nProtected term definitions are a new feature in JSON-LD 1.1.\nThis section is non-normative.\nValues are leaf nodes in a graph associated with scalar values such as strings, dates, times, and other such atomic valu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-048",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Describing Values",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-048-42-describing-values.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "empty"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-049",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Typed Values",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-049-421-typed-values.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Input\nExample 59: Expanded term definition with type coercion\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"modified\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified\",\n      \"@type\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime\"\n    }\n  },\n  ...\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.com/docs/1\",\n  \"modified\": \"2010-05-29T14:17:39+02:00\",\n  ...\n}\nInput\nExample 60: Expanded value with type\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"modified\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified\"\n    }",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-050",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-050-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The @type keyword is also used to associate a type with a node. The concept of a node type and a value type are distinct. For more on adding types to\nnodes, see § 3.5 Specifying the Type.\nNote\nWhen expanding, an @type defined within a term definition can be associated with a string value to create an expanded value object, which is\ndescribed in § 4.2.3 Type Coercion. Type coercion only takes place on string values, not for values which are maps, such as node objects and value\nobjects in their expanded form.\nA node type specifies the type of thing that is being described, like a person, place,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-051",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Warning",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-051-warning.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "JSON-LD is intended to allow native JSON to be interpreted through the use of a context. The use of JSON literals creates blobs of data which are\nnot available for interpretation. It is for use only in the rare cases that JSON cannot be represented as JSON-LD.\nWhen a term is defined with @type set to @json, a JSON-LD processor will treat the value as a JSON literal, rather than interpreting it further as\nJSON-LD. In the expanded document form, such JSON will become the value of @value within a value object having \"@type\": \"@json\".\nWhen transformed into RDF, the JSON literal will have a lexical",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-052",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "JSON Literals",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-052-422-json-literals.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Example 63: JSON Literal\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-053",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 35,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-053-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Generally, when a JSON-LD processor encounters null, the associated entry or value is removed. However, null is a valid JSON token; when used\nas the value of a JSON literal, a null value will be preserved.\nThis section is non-normative.\nJSON-LD supports the coercion of string values to particular data types. Type coercion allows someone deploying JSON-LD to use string property\nvalues and have those values be interpreted as typed values by associating an IRI with the value in the expanded value object representation. Using\ntype coercion, string value representation can be used without requiring",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-054",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Type Coercion",
      "pageStart": 35,
      "pageEnd": 36,
      "target": "../sections/sec-054-423-type-coercion.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Input\nExample 64: Expanded term definition with types\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"xsd\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#\",\n    \"name\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name\",\n    \"age\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/age\",\n      \"@type\": \"xsd:integer\"\n    },\n    \"homepage\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/homepage\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.com/people#john\",\n  \"name\": \"John Smith\",\n  \"age\": \"41\",\n  \"homepage\": [\n    \"http://personal.example.org/\",\n    \"http://work.example.com",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-055",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 36,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-055-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The triple ex1:fred ex2:knows ex1:barney . is emitted twice, but exists only once in an output dataset, as it is a duplicate triple.\nTerms may also be defined using IRIs or compact IRIs. This allows coercion rules to be applied to keys which are not represented as a simple term.\nFor example:\nInput\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"@base\": \"http://example1.com/\",\n    \"@vocab\": \"http://example2.com/\",\n    \"knows\": {\"@type\": \"@vocab\"}\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"fred\",\n  \"knows\": [\n    {\"@id\": \"barney\", \"mnemonic\": \"the sidekick\"},\n    \"barney\"",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-056",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-056-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Keys in the context are treated as terms for the purpose of expansion and value coercion. At times, this may result in multiple representations for the\nsame expanded IRI. For example, one could specify that dog and cat both expanded to http://example.com/vocab#animal. Doing this could be\nuseful for establishing different type coercion or language specification rules.\nThis section is non-normative.\nAt times, it is important to annotate a string with its language. In JSON-LD this is possible in a variety of ways. First, it is possible to define a default\nlanguage for a JSON-LD document by settin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-057",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "String Internationalization",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 40,
      "target": "../sections/sec-057-424-string-internationalization.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Input\nExample 68: Setting the default language of a JSON-LD document\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"name\": \"http://example.org/name\",\n    \"occupation\": \"http://example.org/occupation\",\n    ...\n    \"@language\": \"ja\"\n  },\n  \"name\": \"花澄\",\n  \"occupation\": \"科学者\"\n}\nExample 69: Clearing default language\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    ...\n    \"@version\": 1.1,\n    \"@vocab\": \"http://example.com/\",\n    \"@language\": \"ja\",\n    \"details\": {\n      \"@context\": {\n        \"@language\": null\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\": \"花澄\",\n  \"details\": {\"occupation\": \"",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-058",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 40,
      "pageEnd": 40,
      "target": "../sections/sec-058-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Base direction associations are only applied to plain strings and language-tagged strings. Typed values or values that are subject to type coercion are\nnot given a base direction.\nThird, it is possible to override the default base direction by using a value object:\nSee Strings on the Web: Language and Direction Metadata [string-meta] for a deeper discussion of base direction.\nThis section is non-normative.\nA JSON-LD author can express multiple values in a compact way by using arrays. Since graphs do not describe ordering for links between nodes,\narrays in JSON-LD do not convey any ordering of",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-059",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Value Ordering",
      "pageStart": 40,
      "pageEnd": 41,
      "target": "../sections/sec-059-43-value-ordering.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Input\nExample 78: Multiple values with no inherent order\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\"foaf\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/\"},\n  ...\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.org/people#joebob\",\n  \"foaf:nick\": [ \"joe\", \"bob\", \"JB\" ],\n  ...\n}\nInput\nExample 79: Using an expanded form to set multiple values\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\"dcterms\": \"http://purl.org/dc/terms/\"},\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.org/articles/8\",\n  \"dcterms:title\": [\n    {\n      \"@value\": \"Das Kapital\",\n      \"@languag",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-060",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Lists",
      "pageStart": 41,
      "pageEnd": 42,
      "target": "../sections/sec-060-431-lists.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In JSON-LD 1.1, lists of lists, where the value of a list object, may itself be a list object, are fully supported.\nNote that the \"@container\": \"@list\" definition recursively describes array values of lists as being, themselves, lists. For example, in The GeoJSON\nFormat (see [RFC7946]), coordinates are an ordered list of positions, which are represented as an array of two or more numbers:\nInput\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\"ex\": \"http://example.org/\"},\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.org/people#michael\",\n  \"ex:name\": [\n    \"Michael\",",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-061",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Sets",
      "pageStart": 42,
      "pageEnd": 43,
      "target": "../sections/sec-061-432-sets.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Input\nExample 85: An unordered collection of values in JSON-LD\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\"foaf\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/\"},\n  ...\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.org/people#joebob\",\n  \"foaf:nick\": {\n    \"@set\": [ \"joe\", \"bob\", \"jaybee\" ]\n  },\n  ...\n}\nSince JSON-LD 1.1, the @set keyword may be combined with other container specifications within an expanded term definition to similarly cause\ncompacted values of indexes to be consistently represented using arrays. See § 4.6 Indexed Values for a further discussion.\nThis section is non",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-062",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Using @set with @type",
      "pageStart": 43,
      "pageEnd": 43,
      "target": "../sections/sec-062-433-using-set-with-type.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Example 87: Setting @container: @set on @type\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"@version\": 1.1,\n    \"@type\": {\"@container\": \"@set\"}\n  },\n  \"@type\": [\"http:/example.org/type\"]\n}",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-063",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Nested Properties",
      "pageStart": 43,
      "pageEnd": 44,
      "target": "../sections/sec-063-44-nested-properties.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Input\nExample 88: Nested properties\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"@version\": 1.1,\n    \"skos\": \"http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#\",\n    \"labels\": \"@nest\",\n    \"main_label\": {\"@id\": \"skos:prefLabel\"},\n    \"other_label\": {\"@id\": \"skos:altLabel\"},\n    \"homepage\": {\"@id\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/homepage\", \"@type\": \"@id\"}\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.org/myresource\",\n  \"homepage\": \"http://example.org\",\n  \"labels\": {\n     \"main_label\": \"This is the main label for my resource\",\n     \"other_label\": \"This is the other label\"",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-064",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Note",
      "pageStart": 44,
      "pageEnd": 44,
      "target": "../sections/sec-064-note.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Nested properties are a new feature in JSON-LD 1.1.\nThis section is non-normative.\nInput\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"skos\": \"http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#\",\n    \"main_label\": {\"@id\": \"skos:prefLabel\"},\n    \"other_label\": {\"@id\": \"skos:altLabel\"},\n    \"homepage\": {\"@id\": \"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/homepage\", \"@type\": \"@id\"}\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"http://example.org/myresource\",\n  \"homepage\": \"http://example.org\",\n  \"main_label\": \"This is the main label for my resource\",\n  \"other_label\": \"This is the other label\"\n}\nInput\nExample 90: Defining property nesting - Expanded Input\n[{\n  \"@id\": \"http://exampl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-065",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Embedding",
      "pageStart": 44,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-065-45-embedding.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Embedding is a JSON-LD feature that allows an author to use node objects as property values. This is a commonly used mechanism for creating a\nparent-child relationship between two nodes.\nWithout embedding, node objects can be linked by referencing the identifier of another node object. For example:\nThe previous example describes two node objects, for Manu and Gregg, with the knows property defined to treat string values as identifiers.\nEmbedding allows the node object for Gregg to be embedded as a value of the knows property:\nA node object, like the one used above, may be used in any value pos",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-066",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Identifying Blank Nodes",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 46,
      "target": "../sections/sec-066-451-identifying-blank-nodes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This section is non-normative.\nAt times, it becomes necessary to be able to express information without being able to uniquely identify the node with an IRI. This type of node is\ncalled a blank node. JSON-LD does not require all nodes to be identified using @id. However, some graph topologies may require identifiers to be\nserializable. Graphs containing loops, e.g., cannot be serialized using embedding alone, @id must be used to connect the nodes. In these situations,\none can use blank node identifiers, which look like IRIs using an underscore (_) as scheme. This allows one to reference the no",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-067",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Data Indexing",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 46,
      "target": "../sections/sec-067-461-data-indexing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As described in § 4.6.1 Data Indexing, data indexing allows an arbitrary key to reference a node or value.\nLanguage Indexing\nAs described in § 4.6.2 Language Indexing, language indexing allows a language to reference a string and be interpreted as the language associated\nwith that string.\nNode Identifier Indexing\nAs described in § 4.6.3 Node Identifier Indexing, node identifier indexing allows an IRI to reference a node and be interpreted as the identifier of that\nnode.\nNode Type Indexing\nAs described in § 4.6.4 Node Type Indexing, node type indexing allows an IRI to reference a node and be in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-068",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Indexed Values",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 47,
      "target": "../sections/sec-068-46-indexed-values.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "4.6.1 Data Indexing §\nInput\nExample 97: Indexing data in JSON-LD\nCompacted (Input) \nExpanded (Result) \nStatements \nTurtle Open in playground\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"schema\": \"http://schema.org/\",\n    \"name\": \"schema:name\",\n    \"body\": \"schema:articleBody\",\n    \"athletes\": {\n      \"@id\": \"schema:athlete\",\n      \"@container\": \"@index\"\n⚠\nIn the example above, the athletes term has been marked as an index map. The catcher and pitcher keys will be ignored semantically, but preserved\nsyntactically, by the JSON-LD Processor. If used in JavaScript, this can allow a developer to access a particular athl",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/w3c-2020-json-ld-1-1/blocks/prose-sec-069",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
